# Backup and Restore Testing

## Intent

Every system that holds data we cannot afford to lose must have a **backup** path and a **restore**
path, both runnable as GitHub Actions workflows, and both **tested by hand once a month**. A backup
nobody has ever restored is not a backup — it is an untested assumption. This rule makes the restore
test a standing, scheduled obligation instead of something remembered only after data is already
gone.

## The rule (owner-directed, 2026-07-21)

When you introduce — or discover we are missing — any system that stores data needing backup, all of
the following must be true before the work is considered done:

1. **A backup workflow exists.** A GitHub Actions workflow under `.github/workflows/` that produces a
   restorable backup on a schedule (and on manual dispatch). It must fail loudly (job goes red) if it
   cannot produce a backup — never succeed silently with nothing saved.
2. **A restore workflow exists.** A separate GitHub Actions workflow that restores a chosen backup
   into a **target that is never production** — guarded so a misconfiguration cannot overwrite live
   data. The backup and restore must use matching tool versions (e.g. the same PostgreSQL client
   major version) so a restore can actually read what the backup wrote.
3. **It is registered for monthly testing.** Add an entry for the system to
   `ctf/ops/backup-systems.json` with its backup workflow, its restore workflow, and concrete,
   member-of-staff-can-follow **test steps** that verify both the backup ran and a restore from it
   works. That file is the single source of truth; the monthly reminder issue is built from it.

Do not consider a new data-storing system shippable until all three are in place. If you build a
backup but genuinely cannot yet build the restore, say so plainly and record it as a gap — do not
quietly ship a backup with no proven way back.

## How the monthly test works

- `.github/workflows/monthly-backup-restore-test-reminder.yml` runs on the 1st of each month (and on
  manual dispatch). It reads `ctf/ops/backup-systems.json` and opens one GitHub issue titled
  `Monthly backup & restore test — <Month> <Year>`, labeled `backup-restore-test`, with a checklist
  of every system's test steps.
- The owner works through the checklist by hand: run each backup/restore, verify the restored data is
  real, tick the boxes. If a restore fails, that is a real incident — open a bug issue and link it.
- Optional: set the repository Actions variable `BACKUP_TEST_ASSIGNEE` to a GitHub username to
  auto-assign each month's issue to that person.

## Adding a new system to the monthly issue

Edit `ctf/ops/backup-systems.json` and add an object to `systems` with:

- `name` — short human name (e.g. `Formance ledger (Postgres)`).
- `whatItProtects` — one plain sentence on what data is lost if this backup is missing.
- `backupWorkflow` / `backupWorkflowName` — the backup workflow's filename and its `name:`.
- `restoreWorkflow` / `restoreWorkflowName` — the restore workflow's filename and its `name:`.
- `notes` — anything the tester must know (where backups land, what the restore target must be).
- `testSteps` — an ordered list of concrete actions that prove the backup exists AND a restore from
  it works. Write them so someone who has never seen the system can follow them.

No code change to the reminder workflow is needed — it picks up the new entry on the next run.

## Current systems

As of 2026-07-21 the only system needing backup is the **Formance ledger (Postgres)** — backup
`backup-formance.yml`, restore `restore-formance.yml`. Its full disaster-recovery notes are in
`ctf/ops/formance/DISASTER_RECOVERY.md`. The `weekly-performance-goal-snapshot.yml` workflow is a
product data-capture job (it records weekly metric readings into a table), not a database backup, and
is out of scope for this rule.
